/* Extended portraits */
const BIO = {"Hildegard of Bingen":{"s":"A Benedictine abbess on the Rhine who wrote theology, natural science and medicine as well as music. Her 77 chants and the morality play Ordo virtutum use wide-ranging melodies that break the usual limits of plainchant. Revived on record from the 1980s, she became the best-known composer of the Middle Ages.","f":["Founded her own convent at Rupertsberg around 1150","Her visions were collected in Scivias (1151)","Ordo virtutum is the earliest morality play whose music survives","Canonised and named a Doctor of the Church in 2012"]},
"Guillaume de Machaut":{"s":"Poet and composer, canon of Reims Cathedral and servant of kings and dukes. He supervised complete manuscripts of his own works, which is why so much survives: some 140 songs, 23 motets and the Messe de Nostre Dame. He perfected the formes fixes (ballade, rondeau, virelai) that shaped French song for a century.","f":["The Messe de Nostre Dame is the earliest complete Mass setting by a single known composer","'Ma fin est mon commencement' is a rondeau whose music runs backwards","His Le voir dit tells the story of an aged poet's love for a young admirer"]},
"Guillaume Dufay":{"s":"The dominant composer of the 15th century, trained at Cambrai and active in Italy, Savoy and Burgundy. He absorbed the English 'contenance angloise' and turned it into the international style of the Renaissance. His cantus-firmus Masses, motets and chansons set the pattern for the Franco-Flemish generations after him.","f":["Nuper rosarum flores (1436) mirrors the proportions of Brunelleschi's dome in Florence","His Missa L'homme armé is one of the earliest on that famous tune","Asked in his will for his Ave regina caelorum to be sung at his deathbed"]},
"Josquin des Prez":{"s":"Born in northern France around 1450, he worked in Milan, Rome, Ferrara and Condé-sur-l'Escaut. Josquin combined complex canons with clear, word-sensitive imitation, and his music was the first to be printed widely. Contemporaries ranked him with Michelangelo; Luther praised his mastery.","f":["Petrucci devoted three printed books to his Masses (1502–1514), the first one-composer prints","Ave Maria... virgo serena became the model of the imitative motet","Many works once attributed to him are now thought to be by others, a sign of his fame"]},
"Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina":{"s":"Spent his career in Roman churches, including the Sistine Chapel and St Peter's. His smooth, balanced polyphony with clear text became the official ideal of Catholic church music after the Council of Trent. Fux's 1725 counterpoint treatise, based on his style, trained composers for 200 years.","f":["Wrote 104 Masses and over 300 motets","The story that the Missa Papae Marcelli 'saved' polyphony at Trent is a legend","Pfitzner's opera Palestrina (1917) dramatised the legend"]},
"Claudio Monteverdi":{"s":"Moved from the Gonzaga court in Mantua to St Mark's, Venice (1613). He bridged Renaissance and Baroque, defending his expressive dissonances as a 'seconda pratica' in which words rule the music. L'Orfeo (1607) is the first opera still regularly performed; his late Venetian operas brought real human characters to the stage.","f":["Eight books of madrigals trace the move from polyphony to continuo song","The Vespers of 1610 combine chant with operatic virtuosity","L'incoronazione di Poppea (1643) ends with a love duet for two villains","Became a priest in 1632 after his wife's death and a plague"]},
"Heinrich Schütz":{"s":"The greatest German composer before Bach. He studied with Giovanni Gabrieli in Venice, then worked for over 55 years at the Dresden court, surviving the Thirty Years' War. He brought Italian concertato and monody into German Lutheran music, setting the German language with exceptional care.","f":["Studied with Gabrieli (1609–1612) and later met Monteverdi","Musikalische Exequien (1636) is his funeral music for Heinrich Posthumus Reuss","Composed the first German opera, Dafne (1627), now lost","Wrote his last work, a Schwanengesang, at 86"]},
"Jean-Baptiste Lully":{"s":"Born in Florence, he rose from kitchen boy to absolute ruler of French music under Louis XIV. With the librettist Quinault he created the tragédie en musique and the French overture, and his orchestra set new standards of discipline. He held a royal monopoly on opera in Paris.","f":["Danced with the young Louis XIV in court ballets","Collaborated with Molière on Le bourgeois gentilhomme (1670)","Died of gangrene after striking his foot with his conducting staff (1687)"]},
"Henry Purcell":{"s":"Organist of Westminster Abbey and composer to three monarchs, he died at about 36. Purcell set English words with unique naturalness and wrote for church, court, theatre and tavern. Dido and Aeneas (c. 1689) and the semi-operas King Arthur and The Fairy Queen are the high points of English Baroque music.","f":["Dido's Lament 'When I am laid in earth' is built over a repeating ground bass","His Funeral Music for Queen Mary (1695) was played at his own funeral months later","Britten edited and championed his music in the 20th century"]},
"Antonio Vivaldi":{"s":"The red-haired priest spent decades as violin master at Venice's Ospedale della Pietà, an orphanage whose girls' orchestra was famous across Europe. He wrote about 500 concertos and fixed the three-movement ritornello concerto that Bach studied. He died poor in Vienna, and his music was largely forgotten until the 20th century.","f":["L'estro armonico (1711) spread his style across Europe","The Four Seasons (1725) come with sonnets describing each scene","A large cache of his manuscripts was rediscovered in Turin in the 1920s","Wrote at least 50 operas, managing theatres himself"]},
"Johann Sebastian Bach":{"s":"Organist, court musician and finally Thomaskantor in Leipzig (1723–1750). Bach combined north German counterpoint, Italian concerto form and French dance into a synthesis no one has matched. Little known after his death, he was revived by Mendelssohn in 1829 and became the foundation of musical education.","f":["Walked about 400 km to Lübeck in 1705 to hear Buxtehude","Was jailed for a month (1717) for demanding to leave his post at Weimar","Wrote around 300 cantatas, of which about 200 survive","Fathered 20 children; C. P. E. and J. C. Bach became famous composers","His Brandenburg Concerto No. 2 is on the Voyager Golden Record"]},
"George Frideric Handel":{"s":"Born in Halle the same year as Bach, he made his name in Italy and settled in London (1712), becoming a British subject in 1727. He wrote over 40 Italian operas, then created the English oratorio when opera went out of fashion. Messiah (1742) has never left the repertoire since its Dublin premiere.","f":["The Water Music (1717) was played on a barge for George I","Zadok the Priest has been sung at every British coronation since 1727","Went blind in his last years, like Bach after the same surgeon's operation","Buried in Westminster Abbey"]},
"Domenico Scarlatti":{"s":"Son of Alessandro Scarlatti, he left Italy for Lisbon and then Madrid, serving Princess (later Queen) Maria Barbara. His 555 keyboard sonatas, mostly single-movement binary pieces, are full of crossed hands, repeated notes and guitar-like dissonances drawn from Iberian folk music.","f":["Competed with Handel in Rome: Scarlatti was judged better on harpsichord, Handel on organ","Ralph Kirkpatrick's 1953 catalogue gives the 'K.' numbers in use today","Horowitz and Pletnev made his sonatas staples of the piano recital"]},
"Jean-Philippe Rameau":{"s":"Already known as a theorist (Traité de l'harmonie, 1722), Rameau wrote his first opera, Hippolyte et Aricie, at 50 and caused a storm between conservative 'Lullistes' and his 'Ramistes'. His orchestration, harmony and dance music are among the richest of the Baroque.","f":["His theory of the fundamental bass shaped how harmony is taught today","Found himself on the conservative side in the 1752 Querelle des Bouffons","Debussy called him a true French genius"]},
"Joseph Haydn":{"s":"For nearly 30 years Haydn served the Esterházy princes, isolated from the world and 'forced to become original'. He turned the symphony and the string quartet into the central genres of instrumental music. Two triumphant London visits (1791–1795) and the oratorio The Creation made him the most famous composer in Europe.","f":["Wrote 104 symphonies and 68 string quartets","The Farewell Symphony (No. 45) asked the prince for a holiday by having the players leave one by one","His Emperor's Hymn became the German national anthem's tune","Taught Beethoven (1792–1793), not always to their mutual satisfaction"]},
"Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart":{"s":"A child prodigy toured across Europe by his father Leopold, Mozart broke with his Salzburg employer in 1781 and became a freelancer in Vienna. In ten years he wrote the mature piano concertos, the Da Ponte operas, Die Zauberflöte and his last three symphonies in six weeks. He died at 35 with the Requiem unfinished.","f":["Wrote down Allegri's secret Miserere from memory at 14","His works are catalogued with Köchel numbers (K. 626 is the Requiem)","Was buried in a common grave, as was usual for the middle class","The idea that Salieri poisoned him is a legend later dramatised by Pushkin and Peter Shaffer"]},
"Ludwig van Beethoven":{"s":"Came to Vienna in 1792 to study with Haydn and became the city's greatest pianist. Increasing deafness from his late twenties drove him to a heroic 'middle period' (Eroica, Fifth Symphony, Fidelio) and then to the late sonatas and quartets, music of unprecedented inwardness. He became the model of the composer as an independent artist.","f":["Wrote the 'Heiligenstadt Testament' in 1802 about his deafness","Withdrew the Eroica dedication to Napoleon when he crowned himself emperor","Conducted the Ninth's premiere (1824) and had to be turned round to see the applause","About 20,000 people attended his funeral in 1827"]},
"Franz Schubert":{"s":"A Viennese schoolmaster's son who lived among friends, giving his music at evening 'Schubertiades'. He wrote over 600 songs, turning the Lied into a major art form, and late masterpieces such as the String Quintet, the 'Great' C major Symphony and the last three piano sonatas, most of them unperformed in his lifetime. He died at 31.","f":["Wrote Erlkönig at 18","The 'Unfinished' Symphony (1822) was not performed until 1865","Schumann found the Great C major Symphony in 1838 and Mendelssohn premiered it","Asked to be buried near Beethoven; both now lie in Vienna's Zentralfriedhof"]},
"Felix Mendelssohn":{"s":"From a wealthy Berlin family, he was a prodigy who wrote the Octet at 16 and the Midsummer Night's Dream overture at 17. As conductor of the Leipzig Gewandhaus he shaped the modern concert, revived Bach's St Matthew Passion (1829) and founded the Leipzig Conservatory (1843). He died at 38, months after his sister Fanny.","f":["His grandfather Moses Mendelssohn was a leading Enlightenment philosopher","The Nazis banned his music and pulled down his statue in Leipzig","His Wedding March is played at countless weddings"]},
"Fryderyk Chopin":{"s":"Left Warsaw in 1830, just before the November Uprising, and never returned. In Paris he became the poet of the piano, giving few public concerts but teaching and playing in salons. His nocturnes, ballades, études, mazurkas and polonaises changed piano writing; his relationship with George Sand lasted nine years.","f":["Wrote almost exclusively for the piano","His heart was smuggled back to Warsaw and placed in the Holy Cross Church","The International Chopin Competition in Warsaw has run since 1927"]},
"Robert Schumann":{"s":"A literary composer who first wanted to be a piano virtuoso until a hand injury ended that dream. He founded the Neue Zeitschrift für Musik, hailed Chopin ('Hats off, gentlemen, a genius!') and later Brahms. He married Clara Wieck against her father's will, wrote 138 songs in 1840 alone, and died in an asylum at 46.","f":["Invented imaginary characters Florestan and Eusebius to represent two sides of his personality","Attempted suicide in the Rhine in 1854","His article 'New Paths' (1853) introduced the 20-year-old Brahms"]},
"Clara Schumann":{"s":"Trained from childhood by her father Friedrich Wieck, she was one of the great pianists of the century for over 60 years. She composed a piano concerto, a piano trio and songs, and after Robert's death raised seven children while touring. She was Brahms's closest friend and the guardian of Robert's music.","f":["Gave her first solo recital at 11","Was among the first to play from memory in public","Taught at the Hoch Conservatory in Frankfurt from 1878","Appeared on the German 100-mark note"]},
"Franz Liszt":{"s":"A Hungarian prodigy taught by Czerny, he became the greatest pianist of his age and the first musical superstar ('Lisztomania'). At Weimar he championed Wagner and Berlioz and invented the symphonic poem. In later life he took minor orders, taught hundreds of pupils free of charge, and wrote strange, forward-looking late pieces.","f":["Invented the solo piano recital (London, 1840)","Raised money for the Beethoven monument in Bonn","Wrote over 1,000 transcriptions and arrangements","His daughter Cosima married Bülow, then Wagner"]},
"Richard Wagner":{"s":"Wagner remade opera as 'music drama', with leitmotifs and continuous orchestral texture, culminating in the 15-hour Ring cycle. He built his own theatre at Bayreuth (1876). His harmony, especially the 'Tristan chord', opened the way to modernism, while his antisemitic writings and later Nazi appropriation make him the most contested figure in music.","f":["Took part in the 1849 Dresden uprising and spent 12 years in exile","King Ludwig II of Bavaria rescued him from debt in 1864","The Bayreuth Festival is still run by his descendants"]},
"Giuseppe Verdi":{"s":"From a village near Busseto, rejected by the Milan Conservatory, Verdi became the dominant Italian opera composer for half a century. After Nabucco (1842) he wrote Rigoletto, Il trovatore and La traviata in two years, then grander works like Aida, and finally, with Boito, the masterpieces of his old age, Otello and Falstaff.","f":["'Va, pensiero' from Nabucco became an unofficial anthem of Italian unification","'Viva VERDI' secretly meant 'Viva Vittorio Emanuele Re d'Italia'","Founded a retirement home for musicians in Milan, where he is buried","Huge crowds lined the streets of Milan at his funeral (1901)"]},
"Johannes Brahms":{"s":"Born in Hamburg, discovered by the Schumanns in 1853, he settled in Vienna. Brahms took over 20 years to finish his First Symphony, weighed down by Beethoven's example. His four symphonies, concertos, German Requiem and chamber music combine Romantic warmth with Baroque and Classical rigour.","f":["Played piano in Hamburg's waterfront taverns as a teenager","Hans von Bülow nicknamed his First Symphony 'Beethoven's Tenth'","Never married; his bond with Clara Schumann lasted 43 years","Made a wax-cylinder recording in 1889, one of the earliest by a composer"]},
"Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky":{"s":"A civil servant who switched to music and graduated from the first class of the St Petersburg Conservatory. He blended Russian melody with Western form in six symphonies, three ballets (Swan Lake, The Sleeping Beauty, The Nutcracker), Eugene Onegin and the First Piano Concerto. He died nine days after premiering the Pathétique.","f":["His patron Nadezhda von Meck supported him for 13 years; they agreed never to meet","Conducted at the opening of Carnegie Hall (1891)","The cause of his death (cholera or suicide) is still debated"]},
"Antonín Dvořák":{"s":"A butcher's son from a Bohemian village who played viola in the Prague opera orchestra. Brahms helped him find a publisher. His Slavonic Dances made him famous, and as director of the National Conservatory in New York (1892–1895) he wrote the New World Symphony and urged Americans to build on spirituals and Native American music.","f":["A railway enthusiast who loved trainspotting","Wrote the American String Quartet in Spillville, Iowa","His Cello Concerto (1895) is perhaps the most played in the repertoire"]},
"Gustav Mahler":{"s":"Born to a Jewish family in Bohemia, he became one of the greatest conductors of his age, directing the Vienna Court Opera (1897–1907) and later the Met and New York Philharmonic. His ten symphonies try to 'embrace the whole world'. Rediscovered in the 1960s thanks to Bernstein, he is now one of the most performed symphonists.","f":["Converted to Catholicism in 1897 to take the Vienna post","Consulted Sigmund Freud in 1910 about his marriage to Alma","Was afraid of writing a ninth symphony; Das Lied von der Erde has no number"]},
"Claude Debussy":{"s":"Entered the Paris Conservatoire at 10 and won the Prix de Rome, but rejected the academic rules of harmony. Inspired by Symbolist poetry, Javanese gamelan and Mussorgsky, he freed chords from their functional role. Prélude à l'après-midi d'un faune (1894), Pelléas et Mélisande and La mer opened 20th-century music.","f":["Disliked the label 'Impressionist'","His piano Préludes carry their titles at the end of each piece","Died during the German bombardment of Paris in 1918"]},
"Maurice Ravel":{"s":"A meticulous craftsman known as 'the Swiss watchmaker'. Rejected five times for the Prix de Rome, which caused a scandal, he became one of the great orchestrators: Daphnis et Chloé, La valse, Boléro and the orchestration of Mussorgsky's Pictures. He drew on Spanish, jazz and Baroque idioms.","f":["Was a truck driver at the front in WWI","Wrote the Left Hand Concerto for Paul Wittgenstein, who lost his right arm in the war","Called Boléro 'a piece for orchestra without music'"]},
"Igor Stravinsky":{"s":"Studied privately with Rimsky-Korsakov and became famous overnight with Diaghilev's Firebird (1910). The Rite of Spring (1913) caused a near-riot in Paris and rewrote the rules of rhythm. He then turned to neoclassicism (Pulcinella, Symphony of Psalms) and, after Schoenberg's death, to serialism. He lived in Switzerland, France and the US.","f":["Became a French (1934), then an American (1945) citizen","Returned to the USSR in 1962 after 48 years","Buried in Venice near Diaghilev","Featured on the cover of Time magazine (1948)"]},
"Arnold Schoenberg":{"s":"Largely self-taught, he moved from late Romanticism (Verklärte Nacht) to atonality around 1908, then developed the twelve-tone method in the early 1920s. As a teacher in Vienna, Berlin and Los Angeles he trained Berg, Webern, Eisler and Cage. He fled the Nazis in 1933 and returned to Judaism.","f":["Also a painter who exhibited with the Blue Rider group","Suffered from triskaidekaphobia (fear of the number 13)","Taught at UCLA; George Gershwin was his tennis partner"]},
"Béla Bartók":{"s":"A great pianist who, with Kodály, recorded and transcribed thousands of folk songs from Hungary, Romania, Slovakia, Turkey and North Africa. He built a modern style from this peasant music. His six string quartets are a modern counterpart of Beethoven's. He died in New York, impoverished, after emigrating in 1940.","f":["Travelled to Anatolia with Saygun in 1936 and recorded folk songs","The Concerto for Orchestra (1943) was commissioned by Koussevitzky","His remains were returned to Budapest in 1988"]},
"Sergei Prokofiev":{"s":"A brilliant pianist and provocateur at the St Petersburg Conservatory, he left Russia in 1918, then returned for good in 1936. He wrote the Classical Symphony, Romeo and Juliet, Peter and the Wolf, seven symphonies and the War Sonatas. He was denounced in the 1948 Zhdanov decree.","f":["A keen chess player who beat Capablanca in a simultaneous exhibition","His wife Lina was sent to the Gulag in 1948","Died on 5 March 1953, the same day as Stalin"]},
"Dmitri Shostakovich":{"s":"His First Symphony made him famous at 19. Pravda's 1936 attack on Lady Macbeth of Mtsensk put him in danger; his Fifth Symphony rehabilitated him. His 15 symphonies and 15 string quartets reflect Soviet terror, war and private grief, with a double language whose meaning is still debated.","f":["The Leningrad Symphony (No. 7) was performed in the besieged city in 1942","Denounced again in the 1948 Zhdanov decree","His DSCH monogram motif appears in the Eighth Quartet and many other works","The authenticity of Testimony, his 'memoirs', is contested"]},
"Olivier Messiaen":{"s":"Organist of La Trinité in Paris for over 60 years, devout Catholic and ornithologist. He invented modes of limited transposition and non-retrogradable rhythms and transcribed hundreds of birdsongs. His teaching at the Conservatoire trained Boulez, Stockhausen, Xenakis, Grisey and George Benjamin.","f":["Wrote the Quatuor pour la fin du temps in a German POW camp; it premiered there in 1941","Perceived colours when hearing chords (synaesthesia)","Mode de valeurs et d'intensités (1949) inspired total serialism","His opera Saint François d'Assise took eight years to write"]},
"Benjamin Britten":{"s":"The leading British composer of the 20th century. Peter Grimes (1945) revived English opera, and his operas, songs and the War Requiem (1962) often deal with innocence and the outsider. He founded the Aldeburgh Festival with his partner, the tenor Peter Pears, and was the first composer made a life peer.","f":["Studied with Frank Bridge from age 13","Spent 1939–1942 in America as a pacifist","The War Requiem sets Wilfred Owen's poems alongside the Latin Mass"]},
"John Cage":{"s":"A Schoenberg pupil who 'had no feeling for harmony', Cage invented the prepared piano and then used chance operations drawn from the I Ching. 4′33″ (1952) frames the sounds of the environment as music. His ideas about silence, chance and listening shaped Fluxus, minimalism and sound art.","f":["Long-time partner and collaborator of the choreographer Merce Cunningham","An expert on mushrooms who won an Italian TV quiz on the subject (1959)","A performance of his As Slow As Possible on an organ in Halberstadt is planned to end in 2640"]},
"Philip Glass":{"s":"Studied at Juilliard and with Nadia Boulanger; working with Ravi Shankar in 1965 shaped his additive rhythmic style. With his own ensemble he created Einstein on the Beach (1976) with Robert Wilson. He has written operas, 15 symphonies and film scores, and worked as a plumber and taxi driver until he was 41.","f":["Founded the Philip Glass Ensemble in 1968","Koyaanisqatsi (1982) brought his music to film audiences","Collaborated with David Bowie, Paul Simon and Patti Smith"]},
"Arvo Pärt":{"s":"After early serial and collage works in Soviet Estonia, he fell silent for years, studying chant and Renaissance polyphony. In 1976 he emerged with tintinnabuli, a style of bell-like triads over a melodic line. Emigrating in 1980, he became one of the most performed living composers, with Fratres, Tabula rasa and the Passio.","f":["His Credo (1968) was banned for its religious text","Released by ECM from 1984 in the 'New Series'","An Arvo Pärt Centre opened in Laulasmaa in 2018"]},
"Ahmet Adnan Saygun":{"s":"The most internationally known of the Turkish Five. Trained at the Schola Cantorum in Paris under d'Indy, he combined Anatolian modes and aksak rhythms with Western counterpoint. He accompanied Bartók on his 1936 field trip in Anatolia and wrote the Yunus Emre Oratorio (1942), five symphonies, concertos and the first Turkish opera, Özsoy (1934).","f":["Özsoy was commissioned for the Shah of Iran's state visit (1934)","The Yunus Emre Oratorio was performed at the UN in New York (1958)","Taught at the Ankara State Conservatory and wrote a book on Bartók in Turkey","Honoured as a State Artist (1971)"]},
"Leyla Gencer":{"s":"Born near Istanbul, she studied in Istanbul and Ankara and made her debut in Ankara in 1950. Called 'La Diva Turca', she became a star at La Scala from 1957 and a leader of the Donizetti revival, singing Anna Bolena, Roberto Devereux and Maria Stuarda. She sang 72 roles but made few studio recordings, so her legacy lives in live recordings.","f":["Created Madame Lidoine in the premiere of Poulenc's Dialogues des Carmélites at La Scala (1957)","Sang in the Soviet Union, South America and at Glyndebourne","Later directed La Scala's training school for young singers","Istanbul's Leyla Gencer Voice Competition is named after her"]},
"İdil Biret":{"s":"A prodigy who, with Suna Kan, was sent abroad under a special 1948 law. She studied with Nadia Boulanger, then with Cortot and Kempff. She has recorded the complete piano works of Chopin, Brahms and Rachmaninoff and recorded all nine Beethoven symphonies in Liszt's piano transcriptions.","f":["Played Mozart's double concerto with Kempff","Her catalogue exceeds 100 albums, mostly for Naxos","A State Artist of Turkey"]},
"Fazıl Say":{"s":"Born in Ankara and trained in Düsseldorf and Berlin, he won the Young Concert Artists competition in New York (1994). Say is both a virtuoso pianist and a composer who mixes Anatolian rhythms, makam colour and jazz with Western forms. Works such as Black Earth, the İstanbul Symphony and the Nâzım Oratorio are played worldwide.","f":["Studied with Kemal İlerici and David Levine","His multitrack piano recording of The Rite of Spring was praised by critics","Composer in residence with several orchestras, including the Konzerthaus Berlin"]},
"Nadia Boulanger":{"s":"After her sister Lili's death in 1918, Nadia stopped composing and devoted herself to teaching. From Paris and Fontainebleau she taught hundreds of pupils, among them Copland, Carter, Piazzolla, Glass, Quincy Jones and many Turkish musicians. She also revived Monteverdi on record and was the first woman to conduct major American orchestras.","f":["Told Piazzolla in 1954 to follow his own tango","Taught at the American Conservatory at Fontainebleau from its founding (1921)","Was the first woman to conduct the Boston Symphony (1938) and NY Philharmonic (1939)"]},
"Niccolò Paganini":{"s":"The Genoese virtuoso whose technique seemed supernatural, starting rumours that he had sold his soul. His 24 Caprices remain a test of technique. His Paris concerts of 1831–1832 inspired Liszt to do for the piano what Paganini had done for the violin, and Schumann, Brahms, Rachmaninoff and Lutosławski wrote variations on the 24th Caprice.","f":["Played on the Guarneri 'Il Cannone', now kept in Genoa","Played on a single string as a party trick","The Church refused him burial for years after his death"]},
"Jascha Heifetz":{"s":"A pupil of Leopold Auer, he debuted at Carnegie Hall in 1917 at 16, making famous violinists in the audience fear for their careers. His technique, speed and steely tone set the standard for 20th-century violin playing. He commissioned concertos from Walton, Korngold and others.","f":["Wrote popular songs under the pseudonym Jim Hoyl","Stopped performing in 1972 and taught at USC","Played a 1742 Guarneri 'del Gesù'"]},
"David Oistrakh":{"s":"Born in Odessa and trained by Pyotr Stolyarsky, he was the leading Soviet violinist. His warm, broad tone made him the dedicatee of Shostakovich's two concertos and Khachaturian's concerto. He was also a conductor and a teacher of Gidon Kremer and his son Igor.","f":["Won the Queen Elisabeth (then Ysaÿe) Competition in 1937","Played chamber music with Richter, Rostropovich and Oborin","Died in Amsterdam in 1974 during a Brahms cycle"]},
"Yehudi Menuhin":{"s":"A child prodigy who studied with Enescu, he recorded Elgar's concerto with the composer in 1932 at 16. He commissioned Bartók's Solo Sonata, played for Allied troops and in liberated concentration camps, and championed Indian music with Ravi Shankar. He founded the Yehudi Menuhin School in Surrey (1963).","f":["Played for survivors at Bergen-Belsen in 1945 with Britten","Recorded West Meets East with Ravi Shankar (1966)","Became a British life peer"]},
"Glenn Gould":{"s":"A Canadian pianist whose 1955 Goldberg Variations made him famous overnight. He quit public concerts at 31 to work in the recording studio, arguing that recording was the future of music. His humming while playing and his low chair became legend, and he re-recorded the Goldbergs in 1981, shortly before his death.","f":["Toured the USSR in 1957, one of the first Western musicians after Stalin","Also made radio documentaries, including The Idea of North","His 1981 Goldbergs were released weeks before his death at 50"]},
"Vladimir Horowitz":{"s":"Born in Kyiv and trained by Felix Blumenfeld, he left the USSR in 1925 and dazzled the West with his power and colour. He married Toscanini's daughter Wanda. Prone to breakdowns, he stopped performing several times, returning to Carnegie Hall in 1965 and to Moscow in 1986.","f":["His 1986 Moscow concert was broadcast worldwide","Famous for his own transcriptions such as the Stars and Stripes Forever","Played a Steinway he took on tour"]},
"Martha Argerich":{"s":"Born in Buenos Aires, she studied with Scaramuzza, then Gulda and Michelangeli in Europe. She won the Chopin Competition in 1965. Her volcanic temperament made her a legend, and since the 1980s she has mostly played concertos and chamber music with friends.","f":["Resigned from the 1980 Chopin jury when Pogorelich was eliminated","Runs the Progetto Martha Argerich in Lugano and her own festival","Her 1967 Prokofiev Third with Abbado is a classic"]},
"Pablo Casals":{"s":"A Catalan cellist who discovered Bach's Cello Suites in a Barcelona shop at 13 and first recorded them (1936–1939), making them repertoire staples. A great humanitarian, he refused to play in countries that recognised Franco's Spain. He founded the Prades Festival (1950) and continued to perform into his 90s.","f":["Played at the White House for Theodore Roosevelt (1904) and John F. Kennedy (1961)","His Song of the Birds became a symbol of Catalan identity","Played his last concert at the United Nations (1971)"]},
"Mstislav Rostropovich":{"s":"The most influential cellist of the 20th century, he inspired over 100 new works by Shostakovich, Prokofiev, Britten, Dutilleux and Lutosławski. He was stripped of Soviet citizenship in 1978 for sheltering Solzhenitsyn, and became music director of the National Symphony in Washington.","f":["Played Bach at the Berlin Wall on 11 November 1989","Married to the soprano Galina Vishnevskaya","Returned to Moscow in 1990"]},
"Jacqueline du Pré":{"s":"An English cellist whose passionate playing made her an icon. Her 1965 recording of Elgar's Cello Concerto with Barbirolli became the defining interpretation. She married Daniel Barenboim in 1967. Multiple sclerosis ended her career at 28; she then taught masterclasses until her death at 42.","f":["Played the 'Davidov' Stradivarius, later passed to Yo-Yo Ma","Studied with Pleeth, Tortelier and Rostropovich","The film Hilary and Jackie (1998) dramatised her life"]},
"Maria Callas":{"s":"Born in New York to Greek parents and trained in Athens by Elvira de Hidalgo, Callas became the most famous soprano of the century. Under Serafin she revived bel canto roles like Norma and Lucia as drama rather than display. Her short career peaked at La Scala in the 1950s.","f":["Sang Brünnhilde and Elvira (I puritani) in the same week in Venice (1949)","Lost about 35 kg in 1953–1954","Her relationship with Aristotle Onassis made headlines"]},
"Enrico Caruso":{"s":"A Neapolitan tenor who became the first global recording star. His 1902 records for the Gramophone Company made both his name and the record industry; 'Vesti la giubba' (1907) sold a million copies. He sang over 860 performances at the Met.","f":["Survived the 1906 San Francisco earthquake","Created Dick Johnson in Puccini's La fanciulla del West (1910)","Died in Naples at 48"]},
"Arturo Toscanini":{"s":"A cellist who stepped in to conduct Aida in Rio in 1886, he became the most famous conductor of his time. He premiered Pagliacci, La bohème and Turandot and led La Scala, the Met and the NY Philharmonic. Refusing to work under Fascists or Nazis, he led the NBC Symphony (1937–1954), created for him.","f":["Conducted from memory because of poor eyesight","Was beaten by Fascists in Bologna in 1931 for refusing to play the party anthem","Conducted the first concert of the Palestine Symphony, later the Israel Philharmonic (1936)"]},
"Wilhelm Furtwängler":{"s":"Successor of Nikisch at Berlin and Leipzig, Furtwängler represented the German Romantic tradition: flexible tempo, organic phrasing and metaphysical depth. He stayed in Nazi Germany, protected Jewish musicians and suffered a postwar denazification trial. His live wartime and postwar recordings of Beethoven, Brahms and Wagner are still revered.","f":["Considered himself mainly a composer; wrote three symphonies","Conducted the reopening of Bayreuth with Beethoven's Ninth (1951)","Defended Hindemith publicly in 1934"]},
"Herbert von Karajan":{"s":"Chief of the Berlin Philharmonic for 35 years (1954–1989), and at times of the Vienna State Opera and Salzburg Festival as well. Karajan cultivated a polished, seamless sound and was a pioneer of recording technology. He was a Nazi party member, which shadowed his career.","f":["Recorded the Beethoven symphonies four times","Is said to have influenced the CD's 74-minute length, enough for Beethoven's Ninth","Founded the Salzburg Easter Festival (1967)"]},
"Leonard Bernstein":{"s":"Became famous overnight in 1943 when he replaced the ailing Bruno Walter at the NY Philharmonic. He was its music director (1958–1969), wrote West Side Story and Candide, and taught millions through television's Young People's Concerts. He brought Mahler back to the repertoire and conducted the Ninth in Berlin after the Wall fell.","f":["Changed 'Freude' to 'Freiheit' in his 1989 Berlin Ninth","Taught at Tanglewood for decades","Won many Grammy and Emmy awards, for records and television"]},
"Claudio Abbado":{"s":"Trained in Milan and with Swarowsky in Vienna, he led La Scala, the LSO, the Vienna State Opera and the Berlin Philharmonic (1989–2002). After cancer surgery in 2000 his music-making became even more transparent and intense. He founded youth orchestras and his own orchestras for Mahler.","f":["Founded the European Union Youth Orchestra and Gustav Mahler Youth Orchestra","Was made a senator for life in Italy (2013)","His 2010 Mahler Ninth in Lucerne ended with minutes of silence"]},
"Gustavo Dudamel":{"s":"A product of Venezuela's El Sistema, he won the Bamberg Mahler Competition in 2004. He led the Simón Bolívar Youth Orchestra to global fame and was music director of the LA Philharmonic for 17 seasons (2009–2026). Since September 2026 he is Music and Artistic Director of the New York Philharmonic.","f":["Conducted the Vienna New Year's Concert in 2017 at 35","Won a Grammy for Ives symphonies (2021)","The first Latin American to lead the NY Philharmonic"]},
"Klaus Mäkelä":{"s":"A Finnish conductor and cellist trained by Jorma Panula. He became chief of the Oslo Philharmonic at 24 and music director of the Orchestre de Paris in 2021. In September 2027 he takes up two of the top posts in music at once: chief conductor of the Concertgebouw and music director of the Chicago Symphony.","f":["Recorded all Sibelius symphonies at 26","Plays cello in chamber music with orchestra members","Will be 31 when he begins in Chicago"]},
"Kirill Petrenko":{"s":"Born in Omsk and trained in Austria, he built his career in opera houses (Meiningen, Komische Oper, Bavarian State Opera). The Berlin Philharmonic elected him chief conductor in 2015 and he took up the post in 2019. Known for his meticulous rehearsals and avoidance of interviews.","f":["Conducted the Ring at Bayreuth 2013–2015","Rarely records; his Berlin releases appear on the orchestra's own label","Led the Bavarian State Opera 2013–2020"]},
"Thomas Tallis":{"s":"Served four Tudor monarchs as a Gentleman of the Chapel Royal and wrote Latin and English church music as the liturgy changed with each reign. With his pupil Byrd he held the first English monopoly on printing music (1575).","f":["Spem in alium is for eight five-part choirs, 40 independent voices","Vaughan Williams based his Fantasia (1910) on a Tallis psalm tune"]},
"William Byrd":{"s":"A Catholic who served the Protestant Chapel Royal under Elizabeth I. He wrote Anglican services and three Latin Masses for secret Catholic worship, as well as the first great English keyboard music.","f":["Was repeatedly fined for his recusancy but protected by the Queen","My Ladye Nevells Booke (1591) collects 42 of his keyboard pieces"]},
"John Dowland":{"s":"The great lutenist of Elizabethan England, who failed to get a court post at home and worked at the Danish court. His songs and Lachrimae turned melancholy into an art.","f":["'Flow, my tears' was the hit song of the age","Sting recorded his songs in 2006"]},
"Arcangelo Corelli":{"s":"Lived in Rome under the patronage of Queen Christina of Sweden and Cardinal Ottoboni. Only six published opus sets survive, but they were models for violin sonatas and concerti grossi for a century.","f":["The Christmas Concerto (Op. 6 No. 8) ends with a pastoral for Christmas Eve","Buried in the Pantheon in Rome"]},
"Dieterich Buxtehude":{"s":"Organist of St Mary's in Lübeck for nearly 40 years. His evening concerts (Abendmusiken) and virtuoso organ works drew young Handel, Mattheson and Bach.","f":["Successors were expected to marry his daughter; Handel and Mattheson declined","Bach overstayed his leave by three months to hear him"]},
"Georg Philipp Telemann":{"s":"Self-taught, he became the most famous German composer of his time and music director in Hamburg for 46 years. He wrote in every style and was a pioneer of public concerts and music publishing.","f":["Leipzig's first choice for Thomaskantor in 1722; Bach was third","Godfather to C. P. E. Bach"]},
"Christoph Willibald Gluck":{"s":"Worked in Milan, London, Vienna and Paris. With Calzabigi he reformed opera seria, putting drama before vocal display, and his Paris operas started a quarrel with Piccinni's supporters.","f":["Orfeo's 'Che farò senza Euridice' became one of the most famous arias","Taught Marie Antoinette singing"]},
"Gioachino Rossini":{"s":"The most popular composer in Europe in the 1820s. He wrote 39 operas in about 19 years, then after Guillaume Tell (1829) composed no more operas and spent his last decades in Paris as a famous host.","f":["Wrote Il barbiere di Siviglia in about three weeks","Tournedos Rossini is named after him"]},
"Hector Berlioz":{"s":"A medical student turned composer, Berlioz wrote the Symphonie fantastique (1830) inspired by his obsession with the actress Harriet Smithson, whom he later married. His Treatise on Instrumentation and huge works like the Requiem and Les Troyens made him the great orchestral visionary of French Romanticism.","f":["Won the Prix de Rome on his fourth attempt (1830)","Also a brilliant critic and memoirist"]},
"Anton Bruckner":{"s":"A devout Catholic organist from Upper Austria who came to symphonies late, in his 40s. His nine symphonies, monumental and built in blocks like organ registrations, were often revised; their versions are still debated.","f":["Was famous as an organ improviser in Paris and London","His Seventh's Adagio was written in memory of Wagner"]},
"Richard Strauss":{"s":"A prodigy whose tone poems (Don Juan, Till Eulenspiegel, Also sprach Zarathustra) made him the leading German composer around 1900. His operas run from the shocking Salome and Elektra to the nostalgic Der Rosenkavalier. He served as president of the Nazi Reich Music Chamber in 1933–1935, a stain on his reputation.","f":["Worked with Hugo von Hofmannsthal on six operas","The Four Last Songs (1948) were premiered after his death"]},
"Jean Sibelius":{"s":"Became a symbol of Finnish identity with Finlandia and the Kullervo Symphony. His seven symphonies move towards greater concentration; the single-movement Seventh (1924) is the culmination. After Tapiola (1926) he published almost nothing for 30 years.","f":["Probably burned his Eighth Symphony in the 1940s","His home Ainola is now a museum"]},
"Edward Elgar":{"s":"The self-taught son of a Worcester music shop owner, Elgar became famous in his 40s with the Enigma Variations (1899). He wrote The Dream of Gerontius, two symphonies and the Cello Concerto, and the Pomp and Circumstance marches.","f":["'Land of Hope and Glory' is set to his first Pomp and Circumstance march","Made many recordings of his own music in the 1920s and 1930s"]},
"Giacomo Puccini":{"s":"From a family of church musicians in Lucca, he became the great Italian opera composer after Verdi. La bohème, Tosca and Madama Butterfly mix lush melody with modern orchestration. Turandot was left unfinished at his death in 1924.","f":["At Turandot's premiere (1926) Toscanini stopped where Puccini had stopped","A passionate hunter and motorist"]},
"Modest Mussorgsky":{"s":"A former army officer and civil servant, the most original member of the Mighty Handful. Boris Godunov, Pictures at an Exhibition and his songs use raw harmony and speech rhythm that later influenced Debussy and Janáček.","f":["Rimsky-Korsakov smoothed out many of his works after his death","Repin's portrait shows him weeks before he died"]},
"Leoš Janáček":{"s":"A Moravian teacher who found his voice late, through the study of speech melody and folk music. His love for a much younger married woman, Kamila Stösslová, inspired an extraordinary late burst: Káťa Kabanová, The Cunning Little Vixen, the Sinfonietta and two string quartets.","f":["Jenůfa (1904) only reached Prague in 1916","The Sinfonietta opens with a fanfare for nine extra trumpets"]},
"Sergei Rachmaninoff":{"s":"A pupil of Zverev and Siloti, he left Russia after the 1917 Revolution and spent the rest of his life touring as a pianist. His four concertos, Paganini Rhapsody and Symphonic Dances made him the last great Romantic, scorned by critics but loved by audiences.","f":["Dedicated the Second Concerto to the hypnotherapist who cured his depression","Had very large hands, able to span a 13th"]},
"Alexander Scriabin":{"s":"A pianist-composer who moved from Chopin-like miniatures to a mystical style built on new harmonies. He dreamed of a Mysterium that would end the world; Prometheus (1910) has a part for a colour organ.","f":["Died at 43 from an infected lip","Experienced keys as colours (synaesthesia)"]},
"Bedřich Smetana":{"s":"The founder of Czech national music. The Bartered Bride (1866) became the national opera, and the cycle Má vlast (with Vltava) is played every year to open the Prague Spring festival. He went deaf in 1874 and kept composing.","f":["His First String Quartet depicts the high whistle of his tinnitus","Ended his life in an asylum"]},
"Edvard Grieg":{"s":"Trained in Leipzig, Grieg built a Norwegian voice on folk song and the hardanger fiddle. The Piano Concerto, Peer Gynt and ten books of Lyric Pieces made him one of the most popular composers of his day.","f":["His home Troldhaugen near Bergen has a concert hall","Liszt sight-read his Piano Concerto and praised it"]},
"Ralph Vaughan Williams":{"s":"Studied with Parry, Stanford, Bruch and Ravel, collected folk songs, and edited The English Hymnal (1906). His nine symphonies, the Tallis Fantasia and The Lark Ascending define the English pastoral sound, though his music also faces war and darkness.","f":["Served as an ambulance orderly and artillery officer in WWI","Wrote his last symphony at 85"]},
"Gabriel Fauré":{"s":"Organist at the Madeleine, teacher of Ravel and Nadia Boulanger, and director of the Paris Conservatoire. His Requiem, mélodies and chamber music combine modal harmony with understatement.","f":["Went deaf in his last years, hearing pitches distorted","Called his Requiem 'a lullaby of death'"]},
"Camille Saint-Saëns":{"s":"A prodigy who gave his first recital at 10, Saint-Saëns was pianist, organist, composer, poet, astronomer and traveller. He wrote the Organ Symphony, Samson et Dalila and the Carnival of the Animals, which he banned in his lifetime except for The Swan.","f":["Liszt called him the greatest organist in the world","Wrote one of the first film scores (L'assassinat du duc de Guise, 1908)"]},
"César Franck":{"s":"A Belgian-born organist at Sainte-Clotilde who became the revered teacher of a generation of French composers. His Symphony in D minor, Violin Sonata and Prélude, choral et fugue use 'cyclic' themes that return across movements.","f":["His pupils called themselves 'la bande à Franck'","The Violin Sonata was a wedding present for Ysaÿe (1886)"]},
"Erik Satie":{"s":"An eccentric Montmartre pianist who wrote the Gymnopédies (1888) and gave his pieces absurd titles. He influenced Debussy, Ravel and Les Six, and his 'furniture music' and Vexations anticipated ambient music and minimalism.","f":["Vexations is a short piece meant to be repeated 840 times","Owned seven identical grey velvet suits"]},
"Paul Hindemith":{"s":"A violist and composer who embraced 'utility music' in the 1920s. Banned by the Nazis, he left Germany in 1938 and taught at Yale. In 1935–1937 he advised the Turkish government and planned the Ankara State Conservatory.","f":["Played viola in the Amar Quartet","His Ludus tonalis is a 20th-century counterpart of the Well-Tempered Clavier"]},
"Alban Berg":{"s":"Schoenberg's pupil who brought twelve-tone music closest to Romantic expression. Wozzeck (1925) became one of the most performed modern operas; the Violin Concerto mourns the death of Manon Gropius.","f":["Lulu was left unfinished; Friedrich Cerha completed it (1979)","His Lyric Suite hides a secret love programme"]},
"Anton Webern":{"s":"A Schoenberg pupil whose short, sparse works (some last under a minute) became the model for postwar serialism. He earned a living as a conductor of workers' choirs.","f":["Shot by an American soldier during the occupation of Austria (1945)","His complete published works fit on a handful of CDs"]},
"Aaron Copland":{"s":"A Brooklyn-born Boulanger pupil who created an American sound with open harmonies and folk tunes: Appalachian Spring, Rodeo, Billy the Kid and Fanfare for the Common Man. He also championed young composers at Tanglewood.","f":["Was questioned by Senator McCarthy's committee (1953)","Won an Oscar for The Heiress (1949)"]},
"George Gershwin":{"s":"A Tin Pan Alley songwriter who wrote Rhapsody in Blue (1924) for Paul Whiteman's jazz band, then An American in Paris and Porgy and Bess. He died of a brain tumour at 38.","f":["Asked Ravel for lessons; Ravel reportedly declined","Porgy and Bess was first fully accepted as opera in the 1970s"]},
"Charles Ives":{"s":"An insurance executive who composed in his spare time, using polytonality, quotation and clusters decades before Europe. Most of his music was performed only late in his life.","f":["His Third Symphony won the Pulitzer (1947), 40 years after he wrote it","Ives & Myrick was a leading insurance agency"]},
"Edgard Varèse":{"s":"Moved from Paris to New York in 1915 and called his music 'organised sound'. Ionisation (1931) is for percussion alone, and Poème électronique (1958) was played from 425 loudspeakers in Le Corbusier's Philips Pavilion.","f":["Frank Zappa called him his hero","Most of his early works were lost in a fire"]},
"György Ligeti":{"s":"A Hungarian Jew who survived the war and fled to the West in 1956. His micropolyphony in Atmosphères and Lux aeterna, the Requiem and later the Piano Études made him one of the great composers of the century.","f":["Kubrick used his music in 2001: A Space Odyssey without permission","Le grand macabre (1978) is his only opera"]},
"Pierre Boulez":{"s":"Messiaen's pupil and the polemical leader of the postwar avant-garde. As a conductor he led the BBC Symphony, NY Philharmonic and the centenary Ring at Bayreuth (1976); he founded IRCAM and the Ensemble InterContemporain.","f":["Wrote 'Schoenberg is dead' (1952)","Won 26 Grammys as a conductor"]},
"Karlheinz Stockhausen":{"s":"A pioneer of electronic music (Gesang der Jünglinge, Kontakte), spatial music (Gruppen for three orchestras) and intuitive music. He spent 26 years writing the seven-opera cycle Licht.","f":["Appears on the cover of the Beatles' Sgt. Pepper","The Helicopter String Quartet uses four helicopters"]},
"Iannis Xenakis":{"s":"A Greek resistance fighter who lost an eye in 1944, he fled to Paris and worked as an architect with Le Corbusier. He applied probability, game theory and computers to composition.","f":["Designed the Philips Pavilion for Expo 58","Was sentenced to death in absentia in Greece"]},
"Steve Reich":{"s":"Discovered phasing with tape loops (It's Gonna Rain, 1965) and then wrote Drumming after studying in Ghana. Music for 18 Musicians and Different Trains, about Holocaust trains, made him a leading American composer.","f":["Won the Pulitzer for Double Sextet (2009)","Clapping Music needs only two pairs of hands"]},
"Kaija Saariaho":{"s":"A Finnish composer who worked at IRCAM and settled in Paris. Combining live electronics and spectral harmony, she wrote L'amour de loin (2000), which became the first opera by a woman staged at the Met in over a century (2016).","f":["Won the Polar Music Prize (2013)","Innocence (2021) premiered at Aix-en-Provence"]},
"Krzysztof Penderecki":{"s":"His Threnody to the Victims of Hiroshima (1960) used clusters and new string effects. The St Luke Passion (1966) made him world-famous; he later turned to a neo-Romantic style.","f":["His music was used in The Shining and The Exorcist","Planted an arboretum on his estate"]},
"Witold Lutosławski":{"s":"A Polish composer who, after hearing Cage on the radio in 1960, developed 'limited aleatory', where players play freely within controlled limits. His Concerto for Orchestra and four symphonies are central works.","f":["Played piano duets in Warsaw cafés during the Nazi occupation","Wrote his Cello Concerto for Rostropovich"]},
"Zoltán Kodály":{"s":"Collected folk songs with Bartók and wrote Háry János and the Psalmus Hungaricus. His method of music education, based on singing and solfège, is used around the world.","f":["Wrote a famous Sonata for solo cello (1915)","Remained in Hungary during the war and protected Jewish colleagues"]},
"Manuel de Falla":{"s":"A Pedrell pupil who spent seven years in Paris with Debussy and Ravel. His Spanish works (El amor brujo, The Three-Cornered Hat, Nights in the Gardens of Spain) mix flamenco and modernism. He left Spain after the Civil War and died in Argentina.","f":["Organised the Concurso de Cante Jondo with Lorca (Granada, 1922)","Left the cantata Atlántida unfinished"]},
"Heitor Villa-Lobos":{"s":"A self-taught Brazilian who played cello and guitar in street bands. His Bachianas Brasileiras fuse Bach with Brazilian folk music, and his Chôros celebrate urban popular music.","f":["Claimed stories of adventures in the Amazon that are mostly legend","Led huge choirs of up to 40,000 schoolchildren"]},
"Astor Piazzolla":{"s":"Grew up in New York, played bandoneon in Troilo's orchestra and studied with Ginastera and Boulanger. Nuevo tango, combining tango with jazz and classical counterpoint, made him famous worldwide but enemies at home.","f":["Wrote Adiós Nonino after his father's death (1959)","Las cuatro estaciones porteñas answers Vivaldi's Seasons"]},
"Buhurizade Mustafa Itrî":{"s":"The greatest composer of Ottoman court music in the 17th century, also a poet and calligrapher. About 40 of his reported 1,000 works survive, including the Tekbir and the Segâh Salat-ı Ümmiye still sung in mosques.","f":["His Neva Kâr is a monument of the classical repertoire","Served at the court of Mehmed IV"]},
"Hammâmîzâde İsmâil Dede Efendi":{"s":"A Mevlevi dervish and the leading composer of the late Ottoman classical tradition. He wrote several Mevlevi ayins and many şarkıs, and taught a generation of composers.","f":["Wrote the Ferahfeza and Saba ayins","Died in Mecca on pilgrimage (1846)"]},
"Cemal Reşit Rey":{"s":"Born in Jerusalem to an Ottoman family and trained in Paris and Geneva, he was the pianist and conductor among the Turkish Five. He founded the Istanbul City Orchestra and wrote Enstantaneler, symphonies, concertos and operettas.","f":["An Istanbul concert hall is named after him","Co-wrote popular operettas with his brother Ekrem Reşit Rey"]},
"Ulvi Cemal Erkin":{"s":"Studied with Nadia Boulanger in Paris and taught piano at the Ankara State Conservatory. His Köçekçe (1943) and Piano Concerto combine Anatolian dance rhythms with French clarity.","f":["Taught İlhan Usmanbaş","Wrote a well-known Violin Concerto premiered by Ayla Erduran in Brussels (1958)"]},
"Sviatoslav Richter":{"s":"Largely self-taught until he arrived in Heinrich Neuhaus's class at 22, Richter became the most powerful pianist of the Soviet era. He disliked recording studios, preferring small French barns and churches for his recitals.","f":["Played at Stalin's funeral (1953)","His Sofia 1958 Pictures at an Exhibition is legendary"]},
"Arthur Rubinstein":{"s":"A Polish pianist who studied in Berlin with Karl Heinrich Barth and became the great Chopin interpreter. He performed for nearly 80 years and was known for his joy of life and his memoirs.","f":["Gave his last concert at 89","Refused to play in Germany after the Holocaust"]},
"Wanda Landowska":{"s":"A Polish harpsichordist who revived the instrument with a custom-built Pleyel. Falla and Poulenc wrote concertos for her. She fled her French home in 1940, leaving her library to be looted.","f":["Made the first recording of the Goldberg Variations on harpsichord (1933)","Said: 'You play Bach your way, I'll play him his way'"]},
"Andrés Segovia":{"s":"A self-taught Spanish guitarist who brought the guitar to concert halls and commissioned works from Castelnuovo-Tedesco, Ponce, Rodrigo and Villa-Lobos. His transcriptions of Bach set the standard.","f":["Gave his first public concert in Granada in 1909","Performed into his 90s"]},
"Fritz Kreisler":{"s":"A Viennese violinist with a famously sweet tone and the charm of his own salon pieces. For decades he passed off some of his compositions as works by old masters, admitting the hoax in 1935.","f":["Was wounded in WWI and wrote a memoir about it","Premiered Elgar's Violin Concerto (1910)"]},
"Eugène Ysaÿe":{"s":"The leading Belgian violinist around 1900. Franck's Sonata and Chausson's Poème were written for him, and his six solo sonatas are each dedicated to a younger violinist.","f":["The Queen Elisabeth Competition began as the Ysaÿe Competition (1937)","Also a fine conductor"]},
"Joseph Joachim":{"s":"A prodigy who played Beethoven's Violin Concerto with Mendelssohn at 12, which rescued that work. He was Brahms's closest musical friend, founded the Joachim Quartet and directed the Berlin Hochschule.","f":["Made some of the first violin recordings (1903)","Brahms's Violin Concerto and Double Concerto were written for him"]},
"Itzhak Perlman":{"s":"Born in Tel Aviv and affected by polio as a child, he plays seated. After his Ed Sullivan Show debut in 1958 he became the most loved violinist of his generation, famous for his warmth and humour.","f":["Played the theme of Schindler's List (1993)","Has won 16 Grammys"]},
"Yo-Yo Ma":{"s":"Born in Paris to Chinese parents, a prodigy who played for Kennedy at 7. He studied with Leonard Rose and at Harvard. His curiosity has taken him from Bach to bluegrass, tango and Silk Road music.","f":["Founded the Silkroad Ensemble (1998)","Has recorded the Bach Suites three times"]},
"Dietrich Fischer-Dieskau":{"s":"A German baritone who returned from an American POW camp to become the defining Lieder singer of the 20th century, recording more than 3,000 songs. Britten wrote baritone parts of the War Requiem for him.","f":["Recorded Schubert's Winterreise several times","Also a painter, conductor and author"]},
"Luciano Pavarotti":{"s":"A Modenese tenor whose effortless high Cs made him an opera superstar. With Domingo and Carreras, The Three Tenors (Rome, 1990) brought opera to football fans and billions of TV viewers.","f":["His 'Nessun dorma' became the theme of the 1990 World Cup","Could not read music fluently"]},
"Kirsten Flagstad":{"s":"A Norwegian soprano who became the greatest Wagnerian singer of the 20th century after her Met debut (1935). She premiered Strauss's Four Last Songs under Furtwängler (1950).","f":["Was boycotted after the war because her husband had collaborated with the occupation","Directed the Norwegian National Opera (1958–1960)"]},
"Yevgeny Mravinsky":{"s":"Led the Leningrad Philharmonic for 50 years and premiered six of Shostakovich's symphonies. His rehearsals were exhaustive and his performances of Tchaikovsky are reference points.","f":["Rarely conducted outside the USSR","His DG Tchaikovsky 4–6 (1960) are classics"]},
"Otto Klemperer":{"s":"Mahler's protégé who led Berlin's adventurous Kroll Opera (1927–1931). After exile and serious illnesses, he made monumental recordings with the Philharmonia in his last decades.","f":["Conducted seated after a stroke","Converted to Catholicism and back to Judaism"]},
"Bruno Walter":{"s":"Mahler's assistant and close friend, he premiered Das Lied von der Erde and the Ninth Symphony. Driven out by the Nazis, he worked in America and made warm late recordings.","f":["Born Bruno Schlesinger","His 1938 Vienna Mahler Ninth was recorded weeks before the Anschluss"]},
"Carlos Kleiber":{"s":"Son of Erich Kleiber, he conducted rarely and made few recordings, but his Beethoven 5 and 7, Der Freischütz and Die Fledermaus are counted among the greatest ever.","f":["Often cancelled concerts at short notice","Conducted the Vienna New Year's Concert in 1989 and 1992"]},
"Georg Solti":{"s":"A Hungarian pianist who escaped the Holocaust in Switzerland and became a conductor after the war. He made the first complete studio Ring (1958–1965) and led the Chicago Symphony for 22 years.","f":["Holds the record for the most Grammys of any classical artist (31)","Won the Geneva piano competition in 1942"]},
"Nikolaus Harnoncourt":{"s":"A cellist in the Vienna Symphony who founded Concentus Musicus Wien in 1953 with his wife Alice. His research into period instruments and rhetoric changed how Baroque and Classical music are played.","f":["Recorded the complete Bach cantatas with Leonhardt","Conducted the Vienna New Year's Concert (2001, 2003)"]},
"John Eliot Gardiner":{"s":"Founded the Monteverdi Choir (1964) and English Baroque Soloists. In 2000 he performed all of Bach's church cantatas across Europe and America in one year, the Bach Cantata Pilgrimage.","f":["Founded his own label, Soli Deo Gloria","Wrote Music in the Castle of Heaven, a Bach biography"]},
"Simon Rattle":{"s":"From Liverpool, he built the City of Birmingham Symphony into a world-class orchestra (1980–1998), led the Berlin Philharmonic (2002–2018) and the LSO, and has been chief of the Bavarian Radio Symphony since 2023, now extended to 2033.","f":["Won the John Player Conducting Competition at 19","His Berlin education project was filmed as Rhythm Is It! (2004)"]},
"Andris Nelsons":{"s":"A former trumpeter in the Latvian National Opera, mentored by Mariss Jansons. He led the CBSO (2008–2015), and has led the Boston Symphony since 2014 and the Leipzig Gewandhaus since 2017–2018. In March 2026 the Boston board announced his tenure would end in summer 2027.","f":["Won Grammys for his Boston Shostakovich cycle","The Boston musicians publicly opposed his dismissal"]}};
